{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a film that tells the story of the founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuits that followed. the movie is directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, as a harvard undergraduate who creates a website called facemash that becomes popular on campus. this leads to him being approached by cameron and tyler winklevoss, played by armie hammer, to create a social networking site exclusively for harvard students.\n\nhowever, zuckerberg instead decides to create his own site, thefacebook, with the help of his friend eduardo saverin, played by andrew garfield. the site quickly gains popularity and spreads to other colleges, eventually becoming the global phenomenon that we know today as facebook.\n\nas the site grows, zuckerberg and saverin's friendship begins to deteriorate, and the two become embroiled in a bitter dispute over the ownership and direction of the company. meanwhile, the winklevoss twins sue zuckerberg for allegedly stealing their idea, leading to a series of intense and dramatic court battles.\n\nthe film features strong performances from its cast, particularly eisenberg and garfield, who bring depth and nuance to their characters. the script by sorkin is also noteworthy, with its quick-witted dialogue and insightful exploration of the themes of ambition, friendship, and the consequences of success.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its portrayal of the darker side of silicon valley and the tech industry. the movie shows how the pursuit of wealth and power can lead individuals to compromise their values and relationships, and how the pressures of success can take a toll on one's mental and emotional well-being.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and tho